Fixes an issue introduced by https://github.com/rstudio/bslib/pull/1056

We were using this CSS:

```scss
$bslib-sidebar-fg: inherit !default;

.bslib-sidebar-layout {
  --_sidebar-fg: var(--bslib-sidebar-fg, #{$bslib-sidebar-fg});

  .sidebar {
    color: var(--_sidebar-fg);
  }
}
```

The problem with this approach is that if `--_sidebar-fg` is set on a parent of `.bslib-sidebar-layout`, the `"inherit"` in `.bslib-sidebar-layout` means inherit the _value of `--_sidebar-fg`_ and is evaluated in that context rather than being evaluated as `inherit` inside of `.sidebar { color: inherit }`.

The result was that if a parent sidebar layout uses fg of white, that value would end up passing down to an inner sidebar layout.

The new approach simply resets `--_sidebar-fg: var(--bslib-sidebar-fg)` at each `.bslib-sidebar-layout`. This way, outer sidebar layouts don't affect inner sidebar layouts, unless globally via `--bslib-sidebar-fg`.


This issue is demonstrated in the second tab of `308-sidebar-kitchen-sink` and was found in this run: https://github.com/rstudio/shinycoreci/tree/gha-bea44a3-2024_05_31_05_04-4.4-macOS/inst/apps/308-sidebar-kitchen-sink/tests/testthat/_snaps/mac-4.4/308-sidebar-kitchen-sink.

This PR addresses a few things we noticed in a review of `layout_columns()`:

1. Most changes are around refactoring and adding comments.

2. When `breakpoints(md = 3, lg =NA)`, the best fit algorithm would globally set the number of columns. But because the user provided a column width at the `md` breakpoint, we should stay within 12-unit rows. I fixed this bug and picked better options for a few small `n_items` cases.

3. It seems that the `g-start-{size}-{col}` classes are required in certain cases. This may be resulting from the BS 5.2 to 5.3 change or just an edge case we overlooked initially. This is now fixed by adding `g-start-{size}-1` when adding an item would overflow the current row.

## Auto layout with 12-column-unit rows

https://github.com/rstudio/bslib/assets/5420529/e6606e61-15d5-4f0b-93c3-e3ed780335ac


<details><summary>Test/Demo App</summary>

```r
pkgload::load_all()

random_cards <- function(n = 3) {
  cards <- c("A", 2:10, "J", "Q", "K")
  suits <- c("♠", "♥", "♦", "♣")
  classes <- c("text-bg-dark", "text-bg-danger", "text-bg-info", "text-bg-success")

  lapply(seq_len(n), function(i) {
    idx_suit <- sample(1:4, 1)
    title <- paste0(sample(cards, 1), suits[idx_suit])
    card(
      fill = TRUE,
      fillable = TRUE,
      class = classes[idx_suit],
      card_title(title),
      card_body(
        lorem::ipsum(1, 1, avg_words_per_sentence = 5)
      )
    )
  })
}

current_view <- function(size) {
  sizes <- c("sm", "md", "lg")
  d_none <- paste0("d-", setdiff(sizes, size), "-none")

  span(
    code(size),
    span(
      class = c(d_none, sprintf("d-%s-inline-block", size), "text-danger"),
      HTML("&larr; current size")
    )
  )
}

ui <- page_fixed(
  sliderInput("n", "Number of objects", 1, 24, 8),
  h3("Breakpoints"),
  layout_columns(
    textInput("sm", current_view("sm"), ""),
    textInput("md", current_view("md"), ""),
    textInput("lg", current_view("lg"), ""),
  ),
  uiOutput("cards")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  brkpts <- reactive({
    eval_bp <- function(x) {
      if (!nzchar(x)) return(NA)
      eval(parse(text = sprintf("c(%s)", x)))
    }
    breakpoints(
      sm = eval_bp(input$sm),
      md = eval_bp(input$md),
      lg = eval_bp(input$lg)
    )
  })

  output$cards <- renderUI({
    layout_columns(
      col_widths = brkpts(),
      id = "cards-layout-columns",
      !!!random_cards(input$n)
    )
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

</details>

Fixes the issue reported in #925 by setting the card cap bg using CSS variables in the dashboard mode.

After this change either of the first two properties sets the card header background for `card_header(class = "my-card-header")`:

```css
.my-card-header {
  --bs-card-cap-bg: #fc6a03;
  background-color: #fc6a03;
  color: white;
}
```

## Explanation

To clarify what's happening in this PR, we need to provide a dashboard-specific background color for `--bs-card-cap-bg`. Essentially, instead of `--bs-card-cap-bg: transparent` we want `--bs-card-cap-bg: var(--bs-body-bg)`. The reason is that we don't want a transparent card cap bg to pick up the (potentially) gray background in the dashboard area.

So we do two things:

```scss
:root {
  --bslib-dashboard-card-cap-bg: var(--bs-body-bg);
}

.bslib-card {
  --bs-card-cap-bg: var(--bslib-dashboard-card-cap-bg);
}
```

Setting `--bslib-dashboard-card-cap-bg` gives users a way to change our default. We could also have written this:

```scss
.bslib-card {
  --bs-card-cap-bg: var(--bslib-dashboard-card-cap-bg, var(--bs-body-bg));
}
```

but I was following the pattern we used for other dashboard-page-specific variables.

Secondly, we set `--bs-card-cap-bg` in `.bslib-card`, which gives users three ways to change the background color:

1. Globally via `--bslib-dashboard-card-cap-bg`
2. For specific headers using a class applied to `card_header()` (see example above in PR)
3. Set `--bs-card-cap-bg` in `.bslib-card`.

## Example

<details>
<summary>Example app</summary>

```r
library(shiny)
library(bslib)

ui <- page_fixed(
  theme = bs_theme() |>
    bs_add_rules("
      .my-card-header {
         --bs-card-cap-bg: #fc6a03;
         background-color: #fc6a03;
         color: white;
       }
    "),
  card(
    card_header("Header", class = "my-card-header"),
    "Body"
  ),
  card(
    card_header("Shiny Preset Card"),
    lorem::ipsum(1)
  )
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {

}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

</details>

## Sidebar transition minimum time

The main goal of this PR started out as enforcing a minimum transition time, but the pattern was useful and I decided to apply it everywhere.

Previously we set the sidebar transition duration like this:

```css
.bslib-sidebar-layout {
  --bslib-sidebar-transition-duration: 500ms;
}
```

But this has two major downsides:

1. The (sort-of) "public" property default can't be overwritten, instead we'd have to use `max(var(--bslib-sidebar-transition-duration, 500ms), 5m)` every single time we want to use this value (annoying and obfuscating).

2. The "public" CSS property is scoped to the `.bslib-sidebar-layout` class, which is part of why we can't overwrite it. Users wanting to set the transition duration would end up clobbering our max default calculation.

The new approach creates a [pseudo-private property](https://lea.verou.me/blog/2021/10/custom-properties-with-defaults/) — `--_transition-duration` – whose value inherits the public property – `--bslib-sidebar-transition-duration`.

This means that we can store the default calculation in the psuedo-private variable, and internally reference the private variable directly.

```css
.bslib-sidebar-layout {
  --_transition-duration: max(var(--bslib-sidebar-transition-duration, 500ms), 5ms);

  > .main {
    transition: padding var(--_transition-easing-x) var(--_transition-duration);
  }
}
```

Because `--bslib-sidebar-` is already _a lot_ for a prefix, I dropped it from the private properties. This makes the CSS easier to read where these properties are used. Also, because these are scoped to `.bslib-sidebar-layout`, the class acts as a prefix, so internally we can be more succint.

## All other properties

After doing this for the `--_transition-duration` I decided to go all in and convert the rest of the properties. The end result is that it's now a lot easier to theme sidebar layouts using custom CSS properties on `:root`:

```r
page_fixed(
  h2("Demo"),
  tags$style("
  :root {
    --bslib-sidebar-main-bg: #fff4e6;
    --bslib-sidebar-main-fg: #ff7733;
    --bslib-sidebar-bg: hsl(336 100% 50%);
    --bslib-sidebar-fg: #fff4e6;
  }"),
  layout_sidebar(
    sidebar = sidebar(title = "Sidebar title!"),
    lorem::ipsum(2, 1)
  )
)
```

![image](https://github.com/rstudio/bslib/assets/5420529/94f605fa-24cc-408b-a938-9020aa813c28)

Currently, users could globally use `.bslib-sidebar-layout` instead of `:root`, but it's still tricker to get right than it could be. For example, currently the following doesn't work:

```r
page_fixed(
  h2("Demo"),
  tags$style("
  #this-area {
    --bslib-sidebar-main-bg: #fff4e6;
    --bslib-sidebar-main-fg: #ff7733;
    --bslib-sidebar-bg: hsl(336 100% 50%);
    --bslib-sidebar-fg: #fff4e6;
  }"),
  div(
    id = "this-area",
    style = css(
      "--bslib-sidebar-bg" = "hsl(189 100% 42%)" # turquoise
    ),
    layout_sidebar(
      sidebar = sidebar(title = "Sidebar title!"),
      lorem::ipsum(2, 1)
    )
  )
)
```

In the above, the goal is to set the defaults for a region of the DOM, e.g. under the `#this-area`. To make that work currently, you'd have to use `#this-area .bslib-sidebar-layout`. Also note that inline CSS properties set on a parent would similarly not be passed down, so the inline style on the parent div isn't used by the sidebar layout.

With the pseudo-private properties, both approaches work. By using the private properties, we also have a layer of separation; properties that shouldn't inherit from the cascade don't consult the public CSS properties.

Adds automatic dark mode to bslib docs pkgdown site.

The theme picker and javascript are straight from the Bootstrap docs -- https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/customize/color-modes/#javascript -- with a few modifications to work with pkgdown. In particular, we have to create the navbar theme picker elements, but we also need to get and set the stored theme as early as possible to avoid flashing the wrong theme.

I also included bootstrap icons, tweaked the CSS in many places for better dark mode support around custom elements in our docs, and more.

For reference, pkgdown's syntax themes are stored here https://github.com/r-lib/pkgdown/tree/c9206802f2888992de92aa41f517ba7812f05331/inst/highlight-styles and I picked `github-dark` and copied the styles directly into `extra.scss`:

```scss
[data-bs-theme="dark"] {
  pre code         /* Normal         */ {color:#e1e4e8}
  pre code span.al /* Alert          */ {color:#ff5555; font-weight: bold}
  // etc...
}
```

Closes #720 

The actual fix here is to switch from a `<div style="display:none">` to a `<template>` for the content "wrapper" elements, which will prevent the browser from pulling out that wrapper element out when it appears within a `<p>` tag (then, in the element is connected, we replace the `<template>` with `<div style="display:none">`).

While I was here, I realized that we don't need to set

```js
createRenderRoot(): this {
    return this;
  }
```

on our base element since (almost) all the markup is being provided as children to the custom element (and hence is treated as light DOM). Also, although we do construct markup for the close button inside the custom element (shadow DOM), when it gets shown, it gets pulled out of shadow DOM into the light DOM.

Closes #388

Also changes the background color for `.navbar-default`/`.navbar-inverse` for a few different Bootswatch themes to better match their BS3 result.

## Testing notes

Install `remotes::install_github("rstudio/bslib")`, then run the following app. For each theme, make sure:

* The top-level navbar's bg color matches the "Navbar" section of the Bootswatch theme's site (which is iframed into the application). Note that the 1st navbar has the "default" bg color whereas the 2nd  has the "inverse" bg color


```r
library(shiny)
library(bslib)

ui <- navbarPage(
  theme = bs_theme(),
  title = "Bootswatch navbar",
  tabPanel(
    "Main", 
    selectInput("bsw", "Bootswatch theme", c("default", bootswatch_themes(3))),
    checkboxInput("inverse", "Inverse navbar", value = FALSE),
    uiOutput("site")
  ),
  tabPanel("Other", "Please return to main tab"),
  footer = tags$head(tags$script(HTML(
    "Shiny.addCustomMessageHandler('navbar-inverse', function(msg) {
      var navbar = $('.navbar')
      msg.inverse ?
        navbar.removeClass('navbar-default').addClass('navbar-inverse') :
        navbar.removeClass('navbar-inverse').addClass('navbar-default')
    })"
  )))
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  
  output$site <- renderUI({
    htmltools::tags$iframe(
      src = paste0("https://bootswatch.com/3/", input$bsw),
      width="100%", height="1400px", seamless="seamless", frameBorder="0"
    )
  })
  
  observeEvent(input$bsw, {
    session$setCurrentTheme(bs_theme(bootswatch = input$bsw))
  })
  
  observeEvent(input$inverse, {
    session$sendCustomMessage("navbar-inverse", list(inverse = input$inverse))
  })
  
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```
